morgan: quick check — we're seeing intermittent "Unexpected end of JSON input" from an SSE response in the browser SDK. using fetch + response.body.getReader(), doing: 

const reader = res.body.getReader();
const dec = new TextDecoder();
let buf = '';
while (true) {
  const { value, done } = await reader.read();
  if (done) break;
  buf += dec.decode(value, { stream: true });
  // try to JSON.parse each message when possible
  // often fails because a single JSON object is split across chunks
}

It's causing the client buffer to grow (hundreds of MB) on bad networks. anyone handled partial-json + backpressure elegantly?

jin: yep. two quick things: (1) SSE framing is important — are you actually receiving proper “event/data” lines? SSE should be delimited by "

" between events. you should split on that boundary first, then join multi-line "data:" payloads.

(2) TextDecoder with {stream:true} is correct. the common bug is trying to JSON.parse before you have a full event. keep a small "uncommitted" buffer and only attempt parse when you detect the "

" separator.

sam: also watch multibyte utf-8 boundaries. if you concat decoded strings incorrectly you can break emoji and cause subtle json errors. prefer accumulating raw Uint8Array chunks and decode with stream:true only when you need to search for the event delimiter, or use the decoder on each chunk and treat leftover partial codepoints carefully.

morgan: ok i thought decoder handled split codepoints. so you suggest storing raw chunks and doing a boundary-aware decode? any example?

jin: minimal approach we use in the SDK (pseudo):
```js
const dec = new TextDecoder();
let pending = new Uint8Array(0);
async function pump(reader, onEvent) {
  while (true) {
    const { value, done } = await reader.read();
    if (done) { flushPending(); break; }
    // append
    const combined = new Uint8Array(pending.length + value.length);
    combined.set(pending, 0);
    combined.set(value, pending.length);
    // try to find "\n\n" delimiter in decoded view
    const text = dec.decode(combined, { stream: true });
    const parts = text.split(/\n\n/);
    // last chunk may be partial -> keep raw tail bytes
    for (let i = 0; i < parts.length - 1; i++) onEvent(parts[i]);
    const tailText = parts[parts.length-1];
    // re-encode tailText to bytes to set as pending (cheap but correct)
    pending = new TextEncoder().encode(tailText);
    // protective: if pending > LIMIT then bail/emit error to avoid OOM
    if (pending.length > 1_000_000) { throw new Error('stream buffer exceeded'); }
  }
}
```

jin: the re-encode isn't glorious but it's simple and avoids slicing in the middle of a multibyte char. we cap pending size to enforce backpressure. the server should also send smaller events or NDJSON if possible.

sam: for node-native SDKs we use a streaming JSON parser (clarinet / oboe-style) that emits objects as they complete and applies backpressure by pausing the underlying socket/reader when queue > N. in browser land you can implement similar with TransformStream and a bounded queue.

devex-bot: FYI: internal doc on streaming parsers updated: https://internal.redwood/sdks/streaming-best-practices (check section "SSE framing vs NDJSON"). :link:

morgan: solid, thanks. i'll try the raw-chunk + re-encode tail approach and add a pending-size cap + metric. if server can switch to NDJSON for our endpoint that'd be ideal for reliability. will post results here. :thumbsup:

ops: also consider adding a small server-side sentinel field e.g. "final:true" for big payloads so clients can detect completion even if chunking happens weirdly. saved us in a couple of flaky mobile proxy cases.

morgan: good tip — i can coordinate with backend. thanks all.
